500:                        \qquad ab=2.                        \tag{SZ.18}
501: \]
502: The source Cox chamber overlap for every factor \(U_a\) is (SZ.15).
503: The only remaining comparison is consequently the periodic quotient
504: of these chambers with the exact scalar and selected \(q\)-factor
505: in (SZ.18), including its Mumford boundary and finite-root caps.
506: Its boundary must be matched with the original completed family
507: before the local link numbers of Section 8 can be summed.
508: 
509: In these same target Cox coordinates the two base functions are
510: explicit:
511: \[
512:  v=\frac{s_1t_1-s_0t_0}{2},\qquad
513:  q=(s_1-s_0)(t_0+t_1)-(s_0t_1-v)^2.                         \tag{SZ.19}
514: \]
515: Both are invariant under the physical delta action. In particular
516: the \(q\)-factor in (SZ.18) is the actual invariant polynomial
517: (SZ.19); a product construction replacing it by an independent
518: disc coordinate would omit its singular central divisor.
519: The certificate checks this identity as well as the two chamber
520: line frames. This retained equation is the local input for the
521: periodic Mumford-boundary comparison.
522: 
523: ## 10. Exact two-node base coordinates
524: 
525: There is a direct analytic coordinate map to the two-node conifold,
526: without dropping any parameter unit. Put \(A_0=1-2v\) and
527: \[
528:  \xi(T,v)=\frac{-A_0+\sqrt{A_0^2-3T}}3 ,
529: \]
530: where the square root is the unit branch equal to one at the origin.
531: It satisfies \(3\xi^2+2A_0\xi+T=0\). For \(x=X-\xi\), define
532: \[
533:  R=x\sqrt{A_0+3\xi+x},\qquad
534:  h(T,v)=v^2-A_0\xi^2-2\xi^3 .
535: \]
536: Both square roots are actual holomorphic units on a sufficiently
537: small polydisc. The source equation becomes exactly
538: \[
539:                          (Y-R)(Y+R)=h(T,v).                \tag{SZ.20}
540: \]
541: The two local critical-value curves are
542: \[
543:  T_0=v^2-2v,\qquad
544:  T_1=\frac{1+4v-(1-8v)^{3/2}}8 .
545: \]
546: The latter is the small root of the remaining discriminant factor.
547: Their tangent slopes are \(-2\) and \(+2\); hence
548: \[
549:                  q_1=T-T_0(v),\qquad q_2=T-T_1(v)
550: \]
551: are actual analytic coordinates on the base near the origin.
552: The function \(h\) vanishes simply along both curves away from their
553: intersection. The explicit formula shows its quadratic term is
554: \(-\tfrac14(T^2-4v^2)\). Analytic division therefore gives
555: \[
556:                        h=\upsilon(q_1,q_2)q_1q_2,
557:                          \qquad\upsilon(0,0)=-1/4,          \tag{SZ.21}
558: \]
559: with \(\upsilon\) holomorphic and nowhere zero after shrinking.
560: Replacing \(Y-R\) by \((Y-R)/\upsilon\) in (SZ.20) gives the literal
561: equation \(AB=q_1q_2\). This is an explicit local isomorphism, including
562: the selected \(q_1\) direction, the other \(q_2\) direction and the
563: unit \(\upsilon\). The unit has a holomorphic logarithm on the
564: polydisc, so its restriction to a source-parameter loop has zero
565: winding.
566: 
567: This supplies the two-node base chart for the chamber quotient in
568: Section 9. The global periodic quotient is still not replaced by the
569: affine conifold: its actual \(q_1\) period and the physical delta
570: character remain the data in (SZ.18).
571: 
572: ## 11. Effective scaling and the residual period at the omitted origins
573: 
574: For the actual degree-\(a\) line, the common Cox coordinate \(V\) has
575: auxiliary weight \(a\). Multiplication \(V\mapsto dV\), where
576: \(d=2(-1)^{b+1}\lambda q_1\), is represented after fixing \(V=1\) by
577: \[
578:  (s_0,s_1,t_0,t_1)\longmapsto
579:  (d^{-1/a}s_0,d^{-1/a}s_1,d^{1/a}t_0,d^{1/a}t_1).             \tag{SZ.22}
580: \]
581: For \(a=2\) the square-root ambiguity is exactly the retained
582: \(\mu_2\) quotient, so the effective map is single-valued. Its
583: associated principal-circle line still has charge two. Replacing
584: this map by the \(a=1\) scaling would change that line.
585: 
586: The component labels alone do not determine the quotient links.
587: The source two-node chain has source-period displacement two, while
588: the primitive translation has displacement one. The candidate finite
589: label set is consequently
590: \(\mathbb Z/(2\mathbb Z+b\mathbb Z)\), of size \(b\) for \(b=1,2\).
591: However a combination with zero label displacement remains. Its
592: actual line action must be retained before these labels can be
593: regarded as separate ordinary origin-link boundary components.
594: 
595: This residual action can already be seen directly at \(v=0\), away
596: from \(T=0\). The two-torsion addition formula gives
597: \[
598:                   X(p+G_0)X(p)=T,\qquad U_1^2=T^{-1}.
599: \]
600: Since \(q_1=T\) there, the actual suspension restricts to
601: \[
602:  B_{1,2}=-2\lambda,\qquad B_{2,1}^2=4\lambda^2.              \tag{SZ.23}
603: \]
604: These are actual nontrivial constant line scalings for a contracting
605: choice of scalar. Thus the zero-displacement period has an infinite
606: stabilizer at the omitted Cox origin. The punctured chamber quotient
607: and its Mumford stable locus must be analyzed with this scaling;
608: counting component labels does not identify its compact boundary.
609: This is an additional explicit part of the remaining comparison,
610: not a proof that the desired real bordism cannot be constructed.
611: 
612: The infinity dictionary supplied by root preserves the same actual
613: period, rather than merely its Picard point. With
614: \(v=\zeta^{-2}\), \(X_{\rm old}=\zeta^{-2}X_\infty\), and
615: \(q_{\rm old}=\zeta^{-4}q_\infty\), the target-point formula gives
616: \[
617:  U_{a,\rm old}=\zeta^{2a}U_{a,\infty},\qquad
618:  q_{\rm old}U_{a,\rm old}^{\,b}
619:        =q_\infty U_{a,\infty}^{\,b}\quad(ab=2).               \tag{SZ.24}
620: \]
621: For \(a=2\), the Cartier multiplier satisfies
622: \(r_{\rm old}=\zeta^{-2}r_\infty\); its source and target factors
623: cancel in the conjugation (SZ.17). Thus the original scalar
624: \(\lambda\) is retained in the unified formula at infinity.
625: This check is independent of any eventual periodic-quotient index.
626: 
627: ## 12. The stable-locus end of the residual contraction
628: 
629: The nontrivial residual scaling has an exact real cross-section
630: before any source-flop boundary identification is imposed. On the
631: locus \(s\ne0,\ t\ne0\), put
632: \[
633:  R=|s||t|,\qquad L=\log(|t|/|s|).
634: \]
635: For the effective degree-\(a\) scaling (SZ.22), \(R\) is fixed and
636: \[
637:                          L\longmapsto
638:                          L+\frac2a\log|d|.
639: \]
640: Its unitary phase is the physical delta action and disappears in
641: the orbit quotient. Thus at \(R=\epsilon\) the orbit cross-section
642: before the residual period is
643: \[
644:  K\times\mathbb R_L,\qquad
645:        K=(S^3_s\times S^3_t)/S^1_\delta\cong S^2\times S^3.
646: \]
647: For a constant residual \(d\), its quotient is literally
648: \(K\times S^1_L\). The Euler line is the charge-\(a\) line retained
649: by the nonzero-line gauge. This provides the actual stable-locus
650: alternative to treating an origin with infinite stabilizer as an
651: ordinary isolated quotient point.
652: 
653: There are two comparisons still to do. First this cross-section
654: must be embedded as an end of the full periodic quotient with its
655: selected \(q_1=0\) boundary. Second the successive source-flop
656: chambers must be identified on \(K\) in their actual common normal
657: framing. The literal boundary flop from P3K.23 is
658: \(\tau(h,q)=(q^{-1}hq,q)\); using it here requires computing the
659: arrow from the Cox frames in (SZ.15), not importing a flop count.
660: The scalar stage just computed has identity monodromy on \(K\).
661: Any nontrivial mapping torus must come from that additional chamber
662: identification. No characteristic value of the global return is
663: inferred from this cross-section.
664: 
665: For a single common Cox chamber, the ruling arrow is explicit.
666: Use the same quaternion representatives as P3K.23: \(u\) represents
667: the \(s\)-ruling, \(c\) the conjugated \(t\)-normal coordinate, and
668: \(q=uc\). Then
669: \[
670:  h=uiu^{-1},\qquad k=c^{-1}ic=q^{-1}hq.
671: \]
672: The identity of the common Cox coordinates therefore sends the
673: first-ruling product to the second-ruling product by \(\tau\).
674: The actual \(U\) in (SZ.15) goes from the domain \(t\)-ruling to the
675: target \(s\)-ruling, so its single-chamber arrow is the inverse one.
676: This local arrow does not yet give a power of \(\tau\) around the
677: periodic quotient: the cyclic reset between successive marked
678: source models and the source-period identification must still be
679: included. In particular no bare count of chamber crossings is
680: substituted for that composite.
681: 
682: ## 13. The two Cox frame systems at their exceptional curves
683: 
684: The node-adapted and Cartier-adapted Cox frames can be compared
685: without identifying them implicitly. At the linear level of the
686: conifold, the actual analytic map in Section 10 has
687: \[
688:  \begin{aligned}
689:  \alpha&=(A'+B'-C'-D')/2,&
690:  \beta&=-2(A'+B'+C'+D'),\\
691:  q_1&=-A'+B'-C'+D',&
692:  q_2&= A'-B'-C'+D'.
693:  \end{aligned}                                             \tag{SZ.25}
694: \]
695: Substitute the common Cartier matrix
696: \(\left(\begin{smallmatrix}A'&C'\\D'&B'\end{smallmatrix}\right)
697:  =(s_0,s_1)^t(t_0,t_1)\). The literal factorization is
698: \[
699:  \begin{aligned}
700:  S_0&=s_1-s_0,&S_1&=s_0+s_1,\\
701:  T_0&=t_0+t_1,&T_1&=t_0-t_1,
702:  \end{aligned}
703: \qquad
704:  \begin{aligned}
705:  q_1&=S_0T_0,&q_2&=S_1T_1,\\
706:  \alpha&=-S_0T_1/2,&\beta&=-2S_1T_0 .
707:  \end{aligned}                                             \tag{SZ.26}
708: \]
709: Both constant two-by-two changes are invertible and preserve the
710: auxiliary weights. The Cartier marked point is retained by
711: \[
712:                             s_0=(S_1-S_0)/2.                \tag{SZ.27}
713: \]
714: It is an interior point of the exceptional component, not one of
715: the node-adapted coordinate axes. Thus using \(q_1=S_0T_0\) does
716: not replace the actual marked divisor by \(S_0=0\).
717: 
718: These formulas are the restriction of the analytic map of Section
719: 10 to the exceptional curve, because terms of order at least two
720: in the conifold coordinates have higher normal order after the
721: small resolution. The analytic map lifts to the corresponding
722: small resolution by the displayed ruling; the degree-\(a\) line
723: pulls back to the same degree-\(a\) line on its exceptional \(P^1\).
724: The comparison of their local line frames is a nowhere-zero
725: holomorphic unit after the full analytic image of the Cartier
726: divisor is retained. Formula (SZ.27) specifies its restriction to
727: the exceptional curve; its higher normal terms are carried by the
728: actual analytic map of Section 10 and are not set to zero.
729: One way to verify this last statement directly is to use the two
730: standard \(P^1\) charts and expand the ratio in the two normal
731: coordinates. Its degree-zero restriction to \(P^1\) is constant;
732: the higher overlap terms have coefficients in \(O(n)\), \(n\ge1\),
733: whose Laurent overlap is an additive coboundary. The convergent
734: holomorphic overlap solves on a sufficiently small tubular
735: neighbourhood by splitting the positive and negative Laurent
736: parts. Exponentiating gives the unit comparison.
737: 
738: Such a unit is constant on the exceptional \(P^1\) and descends
739: through the proper small resolution to the normal conifold germ.
740: After dividing its constant value it has a holomorphic logarithm.
741: It therefore contributes no source-parameter winding. The exact
742: conjugation by these source and target units must nevertheless be
743: kept in the period map: consecutive factors telescope, and their
744: remaining endpoint ratio is part of the cyclic reset still under
745: study. This comparison supplies the line-frame arrow between the
746: two coordinate systems; it does not finish that reset.
747: 
748: ## 14. The associated Tate-node chart with its line character
749: 
750: The selected Tate-node attachment must retain the line character;
751: the untwisted fibre product over the conifold would not do so.
752: In the node-adapted variables \(q_1=S_0T_0\), its actual associated
753: local chart is
754: \[
755:  UV=S_0T_0,\qquad
756:  (S_0,S_1,T_0,T_1,U,V)/\mathbb C^*,
757: \]
758: where the auxiliary weights and physical delta weights are
759: \[
760:  \begin{array}{c|rrrrrr}
761:        &S_0&S_1&T_0&T_1&U&V\\ \hline
762:  {\rm auxiliary}&1&1&-1&-1&a&-a\\
763:  {\rm physical\ delta}&0&0&0&0&1&-1 .
764:  \end{array}                                                \tag{SZ.28}
765: \]
766: The two node coordinates are associated respectively to the
767: degree-\(a\) line and its inverse; their product is exactly \(q_1\).
768: The constant scalar unit in (SZ.18) can be placed in one coordinate
769: by its chosen nonvanishing root, without changing the equation or
770: forgetting its period.
771: 
772: Resolve the displayed node in the chart
773: \[
774:                       U=S_0z,\qquad T_0=zV.
775: \]
776: The remaining coordinates are \(S_0,S_1,T_1,z,V\), with auxiliary
777: weights \((1,1,-1,a-1,-a)\) and physical weights
778: \((0,0,0,1,-1)\). In the \(V\ne0\) quotient chart, fixing \(V=1\)
779: requires the \(a\)-th root and leaves \(\mu_a\). Compensating the
780: physical action by the auxiliary parameter \(-\theta/a\) gives
781: the exact four normal weights
782: \[
783:                        (-1/a,-1/a,+1/a,+1/a).               \tag{SZ.29}
784: \]
785: Thus this chart recovers the charged \(C^4/\mu_a\) origin of
786: Sections 8 and 11, with the physical circle unchanged. On the
787: source-stable chart the locus \(z=V=0\) instead describes the
788: fixed surface with the source ruling retained.
789: 
790: This gives an explicit selected Tate-boundary chart in which both
791: the fixed-surface branch and the charged-origin chart can be
792: compared. Its appropriate stable open sets, the consecutive \(U_a\)
793: chambers and the periodic identification must still be assembled.
794: No assertion that all these charts already form a compact quotient
795: is made here. The certificate verifies every weight and the
796: effective quotient action in (SZ.29).
797: 
798: ## 15. Which selected periodic fan the node resolution actually gives
799: 
800: The square diagonal in Section 14 can be read off from the actual
801: torus characters. On \(q_2\ne0\), choose \(S_1=1\), use the source
802: coordinate \(Z=S_0\), and retain the line coordinate \(U\).
803: In the chart \(U=S_0z,\ T_0=zV\),
804: \[
805:                    (Z,U,q_1)=(S_0,S_0z,S_0zV).
806: \]
807: The three rays, in the order \((Z,U,q_1)\), are
808: \[
809:                        (1,1,1),\quad(0,1,1),\quad(0,0,1).
810: \]
811: The complementary chart \(S_0=Uw,\ V=wT_0\) has the rays
812: \((1,1,1),(0,0,1),(1,0,1)\). Hence this resolution has the positive
813: square diagonal \(0\)--\(e_1+e_2\), with edge directions
814: \(e_2,e_1,e_1+e_2\). It is the \(j=-1\) vertical-edge fan of
815: P3K.25, rather than its default \(j=0\) fan.
816: 
817: The other small resolution is
818: \[
819:                 U=T_0z,\ S_0=zV,
820:        \qquad\text{or}\qquad T_0=Uw,\ V=wS_0.               \tag{SZ.30}
821: \]
822: Its first chart has rays
823: \((0,1,1),(1,1,1),(1,0,1)\), and its second has
824: \((0,1,1),(1,0,1),(0,0,1)\). These are the default negative-diagonal
825: triangles with edge directions \(e_2,e_1,e_1-e_2\).
826: All four matrices are unimodular. This is an actual dense-torus
827: character computation, so the two source-loop fan choices are
828: explicitly distinguished by their morphisms.
829: 
830: The fixed-trace determinant for each of these two choices follows
831: from the same associated line chart. In the positive-diagonal chart
832: of Section 14, the fixed locus \(z=V=0\) is the \(O(-1)\) surface
833: with coordinates \((S_0,S_1,T_1)\) of weights \((1,1,-1)\).
834: Along its exceptional \(P^1\), the two normal degrees are
835: \(a-1\) and \(-a\), with physical weights \(+1,-1\).
836: Conjugating the latter gives determinant degree \(2a-1\), hence
837: the actual determinant class \((1-2a)E\).
838: 
839: For the default negative-diagonal fan use the second chart of
840: (SZ.30). Its coordinates \((U,S_0,S_1,T_1,w)\) have auxiliary
841: weights
842: \[
843:                        (a,1,1,-1,-a-1)
844: \]
845: and physical weights \((1,0,0,0,-1)\). The fixed surface \(U=w=0\)
846: is the same \(O(-1)\) source surface. The normal degrees are
847: \(a,-a-1\); after conjugating the negative-weight coordinate the
848: determinant degree is \(2a+1\). Its actual class is therefore
849: \[
850:                            -(2a+1)E.                       \tag{SZ.31}
851: \]
852: Thus the two ordered determinant formulas of Section 5 are now
853: attached to literal fan charts. The scalar-loop invariance proved
854: in P3K.25 does not supply a source-loop comparison: the latter
855: must retain the parameter-dependent source units and the periodic
856: boundary map under study here. No return value is assigned from
857: the determinant calculation.
858: 
859: ## 16. The exact two-step normal-cone reset
860: 
861: The full source reset is already nontrivial on the normal cone of
862: \(C_1\). Retain the marked resolution coordinates
863: \((z,B,D)\), and scale \(B,D\) by a common positive parameter
864: \(\epsilon\). The first translation has limit
865: \[
866:               U:C_1\longrightarrow C_0,\qquad s=-1/z.
867: \]
868: The exact addition formulas, applied twice before taking the limit,
869: give the target curve coordinate
870: \[
871:  z_{\rm out}=
872:  -\frac{(z^2+1)B-2zD}{(z^2+1)D-2zB}.                       \tag{SZ.32}
873: \]
874: In particular it is not the bare normal ruling \(B/D\).
875: Let
876: \[
877:              w=\frac{z-1}{z+1},\qquad
878:              \eta=\frac{B+D}{B-D}.
879: \]
880: Then (SZ.32) says \(w_{\rm out}=w^2\eta\).
881: The first-order base functions are
882: \[
883:                       2v=B-Dz,\qquad T=D-Bz .
884: \]
885: They are preserved by the translation. Solving for the target
886: normal coordinates gives
887: \[
888:  B_{\rm out}=\frac{2v+Tz_{\rm out}}{1-z_{\rm out}^2},
889:  \qquad
890:  D_{\rm out}=\frac{T+2vz_{\rm out}}{1-z_{\rm out}^2}.
891: \]
892: Substitution yields the exact second coordinate
893: \(\eta_{\rm out}=w^{-1}\). Thus the normal-cone torus map is
894: \[
895:                (w,\eta)\longmapsto(w^2\eta,w^{-1}),\qquad
896:                \begin{pmatrix}2&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix}.       \tag{SZ.33}
897: \]
898: The matrix is integral unimodular and its difference from the
899: identity has square zero. The product \(w\eta\), which is the
900: retained projective base direction up to its fixed sign, is
901: invariant.
902: 
903: The compactification data at both nodes are visible in this map.
904: The projective matrix in (SZ.32) has determinant
905: \(-(z^2-1)^2\). At \(z=1,B=D\), and at \(z=-1,B=-D\), its base
906: ideal is analytically \((h,u^2)\): write \(u=z-1,h=B-D\) in
907: the first chart, and use the corresponding signed variables in
908: the second. Each is a length-two ideal, retaining the two
909: successive elementary modifications. They cannot be suppressed
910: when comparing the actual boundary \(K\) with a product marking.
911: 
912: The exact two-step rational map and this normal-cone calculation
913: are saved in code/part3_fourfold/source_zero_translation_normal.py
914: and its JSON/output. The initial proposed bare-ruling formula
915: failed the symbolic check; its code and output are retained in
916: attempts/source_zero_normal_simple_guess. The successful
917: certificate keeps both coordinates in (SZ.33). It still leaves
918: the periodic real-normal clutch, the actual line multiplier and
919: the selected Tate-boundary attachment to be composed.
920: 
921: ## 17. The line multiplier in the normal-cone reset
922: 
923: The projective reset of Section 16 also has an exact retained line
924: coefficient. In the \((a,b)=(1,2)\) sector use
925: \(B=-2\lambda q\,U_1^2\), and let \(V_{\rm reg}\) be the regular
926: \(O(1)\) fibre coordinate on the \(z\)-chart of \(C_1\). Its relation
927: to the canonical meromorphic line coefficient is
928: \(V_{\rm reg}=z\,w_{\rm Cartier}\). Define
929: \[
930:  \widehat V=(1-w)V_{\rm reg},\qquad
931:  p=w\eta,\qquad r=\frac{B-D}{1-w}.
932: \]
933: Here \(w=(z-1)/(z+1)\) is the curve coordinate from Section 16;
934: it is not the meromorphic line coefficient.
935: The exact translation multiplier is \(-2\lambda q/(X_1X_2)\).
936: Taking its normal-cone limit only after multiplying the target
937: and source regular-frame factors gives
938: \[
939:  (w,p,r,\widehat V)\longmapsto
940:                   (pw,p,r,-2\lambda p\,\widehat V).         \tag{SZ.34}
941: \]
942: The base functions are retained:
943: \[
944:                        2v=r(1-p),\qquad T=-r(1+p).         \tag{SZ.35}
945: \]
946: The certificate verifies (SZ.34) from the full rational translation,
947: not from the projective map alone.
948: 
949: The node-divisor frame change in \(\widehat V\) is explicit. On the
950: nonzero \(O(1)\) line, use the two vector coordinates
951: \[
952:                        A=1/\widehat V,\qquad C=w/\widehat V .
953: \]
954: The reset is literally
955: \[
956:        (A,C)\longmapsto
957:        \bigl((-2\lambda p)^{-1}A,\;(-2\lambda)^{-1}C\bigr). \tag{SZ.36}
958: \]
959: Thus on a compact annulus of \(p\)-values the inverse period is a
960: diagonal Hopf contraction for sufficiently small \(\lambda\).
961: This names the actual normal-cone end, including its two
962: multipliers. The nodes \(p=0,\infty\) and the global periodic
963: attachment are still the charts from Sections 14--16; they have
964: not been deleted by this diagonal description.
965: 
966: ## 18. Rescaling the scalar with the source parameter
967: 
968: A direct fixed-\(\lambda\) central quotient retains the
969: zero-displacement period of Section 11. An alternative relative
970: comparison is to use the actual rescaling
971: \[
972:                           \lambda=v^N\lambda_0.
973: \]
974: On the two-node valuation ray, the source and suspension columns
975: then are
976: \[
977:                 h=(2,-a)^t,\qquad g=(b,N-1)^t,\qquad ab=2.
978:                                                                \tag{SZ.37}
979: \]
980: The source Tate parameter has order two, the source-line multiplier
981: has order \(-a\), the translated point has order \(b\), and the
982: selected Hom coefficient has order \(-1\) before this scalar
983: rescaling. These are exactly the valuations retained in
984: Sections 3, 6 and 10. The determinant is \(2N\).
985: 
986: For \(a=1,b=2\), use
987: \[
988:  U_N=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\-2&1\end{pmatrix}
989:      \begin{pmatrix}-1&0\\0&1\end{pmatrix}
990:      \begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
991:  V_N=\begin{pmatrix}1&N-1\\0&1\end{pmatrix}.
992: \]
993: For \(a=2,b=1\), use
994: \[
995:  U_N=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix}
996:      \begin{pmatrix}1&0\\-(N-1)&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
997:  V_N=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}
998:      \begin{pmatrix}1&-2\\0&1\end{pmatrix}.
999: \]
1000: All these matrices are unimodular and give
1001: \[
1002:  U_N\begin{pmatrix}2&b\\-a&N-1\end{pmatrix}V_N
1003:                          =\operatorname{diag}(1,2N).       \tag{SZ.38}
1004: \]
1005: Consequently the lattice quotient for \(N>0\) is
1006: \(\mathbb Z/(2N)\); for \(N=0\) it has the residual free
1007: \(\mathbb Z\). These are lattice statements. The finite quotient
1008: has not yet been identified with a collection of origin-link
1009: boundary components.
1010: 
1011: For the currently marked varying-source family, use the stronger
1012: choice \(N=M\), or a positive multiple of \(M\), and take the
1013: literal one-gamma parameter \(r=v^{N/M}r_0\). All finite-cap powers
1014: are then integral in \(v\). The rescaled loop contains \(N/M\)
1015: full one-gamma scalar turns in addition to the source turn. At
1016: the fixed comparison source these are \(bN/M\) of the proved
1017: shorter loops of P3K.35. No class in \(\Theta_7\) is divided.
1018: 
1019: Divisibility by \(M/b\) is the arithmetic lower requirement at a
1020: fixed source. It is not used here as a global varying-source
1021: endpoint theorem: the rational \(b\)-th root \(g_0(t,v)\) in
1022: P3K.35 must still be tracked around the source puncture before
1023: that smaller rescaling can be asserted. The actual finite-cap
1024: root maps and their source-parameter units remain part of the
1025: relative comparison.
1026: 
1027: The symbolic Smith identities and512 positive-\(N\) evaluations
1028: are saved in code/part3_fourfold/source_zero_rescaled_periods.py
1029: and its JSON/output. The remaining work is the compact relative
1030: periodic chart construction and its oriented boundary map.
